# Effects and Workflows

```elixir
System.put_env("AL_MNESIA_DISTRIBUTED", "false")

System.put_env(
  "AL_MNESIA_DIR",
  Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "al_livebook_#{:erlang.unique_integer([:positive])}")
)

Mix.install(
  [{:al, github: "anoma/AL-Ex"}],
  config: [
    al: [
      transaction_programs: [
        AL.TransactionProgram.Bootstrap,
        AL.TransactionProgram.PackageSystem
      ],
      package_channels: [{:builtin, {:priv, "packages"}}],
      package_environment: [],
      edge_providers: [],
      natives: []
    ]
  ]
)
```

```elixir
use AL
```

## Why workflows exist

An AL transaction can atomically inspect and change durable AL objects. It cannot safely keep that transaction open while waiting for a file, socket, HTTP service, or other part of the host: the operation may block, cannot be rolled back, and must not be repeated just because the transaction retries.

An effect describes that external operation inside the transaction but performs it only after the transaction commits. Its result therefore arrives after the original transaction has finished.

A workflow connects these pieces. It expresses one larger operation as a sequence of explicit transactions separated by effects. This lets AL create durable state, perform host work, continue from the result, wait for several effects, and handle failure without pretending the whole sequence is one atomic transaction.

```text
transaction -> effect -> host -> transaction -> ...
```

## Define an effect provider

An effect provider is an Elixir module registered under a name. Here it can uppercase text immediately, reject an operation, or leave an operation pending for us to complete later.

```elixir
defmodule EffectsLivebook.Provider do
  @behaviour AL.Edge

  @impl AL.Edge
  def __edge_provider__, do: :livebook

  def observe(pid), do: :persistent_term.put({__MODULE__, :observer}, pid)

  @impl AL.Edge
  def execute(:uppercase, [text], _context) do
    {:ok, String.upcase(text)}
  end

  def execute(:reject, [reason], _context) do
    {:error, reason}
  end

  def execute(:wait, [value], context) do
    send(:persistent_term.get({__MODULE__, :observer}), {:edge_pending, context, value})
    :pending
  end
end

:ok = AL.Edge.register(EffectsLivebook.Provider)
:ok = EffectsLivebook.Provider.observe(self())
```

Applications normally register providers at startup:

```text
config :al,
  edge_providers: [MyApplication.Edge.HTTP, MyApplication.Edge.TCP]
```

A provider returns `{:ok, value}`, `{:error, reason}`, or `:pending`. A pending provider keeps the supplied context and later calls `AL.Edge.complete(context, outcome)`.

## Put the effect behind an AL object

AL code calls an ordinary object method. That method describes the host operation with `emit_effect/4` and names the method that will receive its outcome.

```elixir
{:atomic, _} =
  run do
    defclass :text_job,
      super: :object,
      redef: true,
      ivars: [input: [], outcome: []] do
      defmethod(:init, [self, args, self]) do
        get(args, :input, input)
        set_slots(self, %{input: input, outcome: :none})
      end

      defmethod(:uppercase, [self]) do
        get(self, :input, input)
        emit_effect(:livebook, :uppercase, [input], {self, :resolved, []})
      end

      defmethod(:reject, [self, reason]) do
        emit_effect(:livebook, :reject, [reason], {self, :resolved, []})
      end

      defmethod(:wait, [self]) do
        get(self, :input, input)
        emit_effect(:livebook, :wait, [input], {self, :resolved, []})
      end

      defmethod(:resolved, [self, _effect_id, outcome]) do
        set_slot(self, :outcome, outcome)
      end
    end
  end
```

The callback receives the effect id and the provider outcome. It runs in a new transaction after the host operation completes.

## Compose transactions with a workflow

Each `transaction` block is one committed step. Values needed by later steps, such as `job`, are retained by the durable workflow object.

```elixir
{:atomic, _} =
  run do
    defworkflow :uppercase_text, [text], outputs: [result] do
      transaction do
        new(:text_job, %{input: text}, job)
        uppercase(job)
      end

      transaction do
        get(job, :outcome, {:ok, result})
      end
    end
  end

{:ok, workflow} = AL.workflow(:uppercase_text, ["hello edge"])
AL.await_workflow(workflow)
```

`AL.workflow/3` starts the workflow and returns its object name. `AL.await_workflow/2` returns the output map when the final step has bound every declared output.

Effects do not run if their transaction aborts. When a transaction commits several effects, the workflow waits for all of them before starting its next transaction.

## Pending effects form a barrier

```elixir
{:atomic, _} =
  run do
    defworkflow :two_waits, [], outputs: [first, second] do
      transaction do
        new(:text_job, %{input: :first}, first_job)
        new(:text_job, %{input: :second}, second_job)
        wait(first_job)
        wait(second_job)
      end

      transaction do
        get(first_job, :outcome, {:ok, first})
        get(second_job, :outcome, {:ok, second})
      end
    end
  end

{:ok, waiting_workflow} = AL.workflow(:two_waits, [])

pending =
  for _ <- 1..2, into: %{} do
    receive do
      {:edge_pending, context, label} -> {label, context}
    after
      1_000 -> raise "effect did not reach the provider"
    end
  end

:ok = AL.Edge.complete(pending.first, {:ok, :one})
:ok = AL.Edge.complete(pending.second, {:ok, :two})

AL.await_workflow(waiting_workflow)
```

## Handle failure in AL

An effect error is data. A later transaction can match it and choose another logical path.

```elixir
{:atomic, _} =
  run do
    defworkflow :recover_rejection, [reason], outputs: [result] do
      transaction do
        new(:text_job, %{input: :unused}, job)
        reject(job, reason)
      end

      transaction do
        get(job, :outcome, outcome)

        alternative(
          [unify(outcome, {:ok, result})],
          [unify(outcome, {:error, error}), unify(result, {:recovered, error})]
        )
      end
    end
  end

{:ok, recovering_workflow} = AL.workflow(:recover_rejection, [:unavailable])
AL.await_workflow(recovering_workflow)
```

If no path in the next transaction accepts the outcome, the workflow becomes `:blocked`. Its object retains the condition for inspection instead of silently disappearing.

```elixir
{:atomic, _} =
  run do
    defworkflow :require_success, [], outputs: [result] do
      transaction do
        new(:text_job, %{input: :unused}, job)
        reject(job, :unavailable)
      end

      transaction do
        get(job, :outcome, {:ok, result})
      end
    end
  end

{:ok, blocked_workflow} = AL.workflow(:require_success, [])
AL.await_workflow(blocked_workflow)
```

That is the complete division of responsibility: AL objects expose useful operations, effect providers perform host work, and workflows explicitly compose the transactions around it.
